// Per-tick performance ceilings for `simrunner bench`, checked by
// `scripts/check-perf.sh`.
//
// WHAT THESE ARE
//
// Cliff detectors: an accidentally quadratic loop, a per-tick allocation that
// was not there yesterday, a linear scan that became a scan over every
// entity. They sit at roughly three times the p99 a developer machine
// produces, because a shared CI runner is slower and noisier and a tighter
// gate fails on neighbours rather than on regressions.
//
// Measured variance on a developer machine across three runs of three
// repeats: p99 spread was 1.1x-1.2x. CI will be worse.
//
// HOW THESE RELATE TO docs/04 §12
//
// §12 budgets the simulation at 19 ms of the 50 ms tick at 200 population
// and 400 entities, row by row: 6 ms pathfinding, 3 movement, 3 combat and
// orders, 2 economy, 1 fog, 4 the opponents. `simrunner bench --stats`
// prints the tick by those phases; the ceilings below are on the whole
// tick. `docs/09` §8 has the phase table and the history.
//
// The scenarios (tools/simrunner/src/scenarios.rs):
//
// - `economy-2p`: a settled two-player economy, the quiet case.
// - `marching-8p`: eight players keeping ~320 units under way at once, with
//   no combat and no AI: the pathfinding and separation load, deliberately
//   unkind to flow fields (each order picks a random handful of a player's
//   units scattered across the map, so a group spans about ten sectors).
// - `crowded`: economy and marching together on a 200-tile map, with
//   crowds spawned: the busiest thing the scripted players produce.
// - `battle-400`: two hundred soldiers a side, sent in: §11's "400 units
//   fighting", the combat and separation load.
// - `opponents-8p`: eight Hard computer opponents on a 168-tile map, each
//   handed forty units at tick 0, their thinking timed as part of the
//   tick: the §12 world with everything on.
//
// HISTORY
//
// Every number before M7 chunk 5 included a full hash of the state after
// each tick, because the bench had run replays through `Replay::run`,
// which hashes for the digest. M7 chunk 5 made the bench step the
// simulation itself, made the fog of war incremental (it had been the
// largest phase everywhere, 50-55% of an eight-player tick) and bucketed
// the nearest-enemy search; the ceilings below were reset to three times
// the p99 the tick alone then cost, on a 2.1 GHz Xeon of the shared
// runner's class. Before that, M4 chunk 1's flow fields had brought
// `marching-8p` from 6.5 ms to 2.4 ms p99 on a developer machine, and the
// ceilings were lowered then in the same commit.
//
// The ceilings are still deliberately loose. The useful signal is the
// trend, printed on every run. When a system lands that makes a scenario
// meaningfully slower, lower or raise the ceiling to three times the new
// number in the same commit and say what bought the time — a ceiling that
// is never revisited stops being a gate.
//
// Verify §12 properly on known hardware at milestone review (F4 in the
// app shows the same phases live). A shared runner cannot answer that
// question and should not pretend to.
(
    scenarios: [
        (
            name: "economy-2p",
            note: "a settled two-player economy: gathering, drop-off, building",
            max_p99_us: 250.0,
        ),
        (
            name: "marching-8p",
            note: "eight players pathing ~320 units at once; the docs/04 §5 load",
            max_p99_us: 7000.0,
        ),
        (
            name: "crowded",
            note: "economy and marching together on a large map",
            max_p99_us: 6000.0,
        ),
        (
            name: "battle-400",
            note: "four hundred soldiers fighting; the docs/04 §11 combat load",
            max_p99_us: 1100.0,
        ),
        (
            name: "opponents-8p",
            note: "eight Hard opponents on a full world, thinking included; the docs/04 §12 world",
            max_p99_us: 3000.0,
        ),
    ],
)
